ay the  Patriots force him to sell the way that  they forced G rich and so they didn't  force Jerry Richardson to sell he chose  to do it on his own and there's no  reason to think the NFL would have even  had the power to force him to sell  that's what Richardson chose to do once  it came to light that there had been  some workplace issues that resulted in  settlements that had confidentiality  clauses some of the facts came out it  was embarrassing to Jerry Richardson so  he just said you know what I'm gonna  sell anyway at some point let's just do  it now  so it's there's a similarity there  there's a similar threat the idea that  if Robert Kraft is thinking you know  what at some point I was going to hand  the reins to Jonathan I may as well just  do it now and if that becomes part of  the punishment that he imposes on  himself to take because again that's  what Richardson did for Roger Goodell  Richardson didn't put Roger Goodell in a  tough spot and this would be a way to  not put Roger Goodell in a tough spot  as it relates to coming up with some  sort of a discipline to be imposed  personally on Robert Kraft and that  wouldn't shock me if that's the outcome  again for now though the indication is  they're gonna fight this thing and we'll  see if that fight plays out the court  date the first court date it doesn't  have to be there for this his lawyer  will be there on April 24 that just  shows you how slow this process is big  cat I mean here we are it's February 26  it's gonna be two months before there's  even that first moment where you walk  into court and get scheduling and do the  My Cousin Vinny guilty or not guilty  routine and and and off it goes and who  knows how long it takes in the court  system before it finally gets to a  conclusion yeah and reading all the you  know everything I've read since the  report came out on Friday is that Robert  Kraft will never have to probably appear  in court so that saves him that  embarrassment of a mug shot and a perp  walk and all that stuff so it's going to  be one of those stories that will always  linger but I think that the big initial  shock has kind of waned off and we won't  have that moment where you know it will  be it will be like you said a court  filing that will then somehow get to the  press and people will read about it but  it won't be that big splash that you'll  see where you see Robert Kraft walking  into court or you see some big moment in  kort it's not none of that's gonna  happen it's gonna all take place behind  closed doors and we're gonna hear about  it but it's gonna be two months down the  line unless he chooses to fight this  thing and at some point there will be a  trial it may be a year from now it may  be two years from now who knows but if  he decides I am gonna push this to the  limit I can prove my innocence or there  there's you know there were some other  argument that can be made it's a high  standard in in a criminal justice  proceeding to prove that guilt beyond a  reasonable doubt if he chooses to do  that and he has every right to do it  then this could take some time and then  at some point he would have to go to  court and and presumably would wait and  he doesn't have to testify that's one of  the things we all learned from watching  TV and very rarely does a criminal  defendant get on the stand and testify  because the chances are you gonna get  ripped to shreds by the prosecutor on  cross-examination but at some point if  he does decide he wants full exoneration  and there isn't a deal to be struck then  there will be a court proceeding but I  don't think it gets to that but if it  does get to that I think it's way down  the line  hi I'm Mike Tirico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from NBC  Sports

